    "findingId": "F3",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Confirmed in `MochiSseStream.send` (Mochi.ts:1386-1405): `id` and `event` are interpolated with zero validation (`frame += `id: ${opts.id}\\n``), and the body is split with `data.split('\\n')` — LF only. The HTML spec's event-stream parser splits lines on CRLF, a lone LF, *or* a lone CR, so `send('hello\\r\\rdata: forged')` puts `data: hello\\r\\rdata: forged\\n\\n` on the wire, which EventSource parses as two dispatched events ('hello', then 'forged'); a bare `\\n` in `id`/`event` is even cheaper and lets an attacker set an arbitrary event type or poison `Last-Event-ID`. Nothing downstream normalizes it: the string chunks go straight through `controller.enqueue` into `new Response(body)`. The framework owns the framing (it already splits on `\\n`, so newline handling was considered and CR was simply missed) and docs/110-server-sent-events.md:39-44 documents `send(data, {event, id})` with no sanitization requirement, so this is squarely a framework defect rather than an app責任. Exploitation requires an app to pass attacker-influenced text into `send` — realistic for a chat/notification feed, and the mitigating detail is that the documented `JSON.stringify(...)` idiom escapes CR as `\\\\r`, so every in-repo SSE route (site/demos streams, queue, admin stock) is immune. Keeping MEDIUM: the primitive is real and the consequence when triggered (forged server-authored events in other users' streams) is meaningful."
