    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Verified. The `Mochi.ws()` handler (Mochi.ts:1262-1328) calls `buildRequestContext(..., { kind: 'ws' })`, and `KIND_POLICY.ws` sets `csrf: false` (requestSetup.ts:66). Even with csrf enabled, `csrfCheckDefault` would pass: it returns `null` unless the method is in `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_METHODS` (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) *and* the content type is form-like (csrf.ts:126-131), and a WebSocket handshake is a GET. The handshake then reaches `server.upgrade(req, {...})` at 1310 with the victim's cookies attached and no comparison of `Origin` against `resolveExpectedOrigin()`. The route is matched by Bun's router, so `composedFetch` (the one other `csrfCheck` call site) never sees it either. Browsers apply neither same-origin policy nor preflight to WebSockets, so `new WebSocket('wss://victim/ws/chat')` from any attacker page connects as the logged-in victim with full bidirectional access — textbook cross-site WebSocket hijacking. The documentation steers users straight into it: docs/100-websocket-routes.md:73 says 'Reject unauthenticated sockets in `upgrade`, not `message`', and :81 says 'getRequestContext() works inside `upgrade`, so cookies and getClientAddress() are available there' — cookie auth in `upgrade` succeeds for a cross-origin attacker. The doc's own code sample happens to use a custom header (`x-user-id`), which a browser cannot set on a WS handshake, so the example itself is safe; the hazard is the cookie path the prose advertises. MEDIUM is right: no framework-side mitigation exists, but exploitation depends on the app choosing cookie-based auth in `upgrade`."
