    "reasoning": "Every code claim checks out. Mochi.ts:1816-1823 registers `/__mochi/health/memory` on `process.env.MOCHI_MEMORY_PROBE === '1'` alone, as a bare handler: it does not go through `buildRequestContext`, so neither the protection gate nor middleware nor any rate limiter applies, and it calls `Bun.gc(true)` — a synchronous full collection on Bun's single JS thread — before returning `process.memoryUsage()`. The asymmetry the finding highlights is real: every sibling internal route is `development && …` (`debugBarEnabled` at 503 for buildClientStatsRoutes/buildPageCacheAdminRoutes, `emailViewerEnabled` at 619 for the email viewer), while this one has no mode check at all. Downgrading MEDIUM→BUG on reachability, which I checked rather than assumed: a repo-wide grep shows `MOCHI_MEMORY_PROBE=1` is set in exactly one place — `Dockerfile.memtest:53`, a dedicated memory-regression harness image that runs `NODE_ENV=development` and is described as 'intended for an unattended multi-day run on a dedicated box'. It is not the deploy image (`Dockerfile` / `Dockerfile.production`), appears in no `.env.example`, and the project's own notes list `MOCHI_MEMORY_PROBE` among the flag-gated dev surfaces. So the finding's 'plausible for the flag to survive into a real deploy' is a stretch: reaching the exposure requires an operator to deliberately set a non-default diagnostic env var in production. It remains a genuine hardening gap worth fixing (add `development &&`, or decouple the forced GC from the measurement), just not a MEDIUM-severity exposure in anything the project ships."
