    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Both halves verified. runtime/publicDir.ts:95-104 assigns `routes[routeKey] = Bun.file(diskPath)` whenever no `publicRouteGuard` is present — a static Bun route value, served natively by the router, so the request never enters `composedFetch`. Since `handle`, `filterResponseHeaders`, the CSRF check, the rate limiter and the `request` event all live inside `composedFetch`/`buildRequestContext`, none of them run for any `/public/**` URL. A `publicRouteGuard` exists only when `protection.protectFiles` is on (Mochi.ts:1845-1858), and the framework's own comment at 1843 confirms the design intent ('publicDir files bypass buildRequestContext (they're raw Bun routes), so protection reaches them through this guard'). The documentation is what makes this security-relevant rather than merely surprising: docs/160-serve-options.md:33 states public-dir files 'keep Bun's default static-route headers. To override, mutate `response.headers` in a `handle` middleware' — a middleware adding CSP/HSTS/X-Frame-Options to static files silently does nothing — and the staticDirs warning at :109 redirects readers wanting 'auth, custom headers, logging' to `publicDir`, where only the protection guard (not auth middleware, not logging) actually applies. By contrast `Mochi.file()` and `staticDirs` middleware-exemption are both correctly documented, so this is specifically a publicDir doc gap. Keeping MEDIUM: the framework behavior is intentional, but an operator following the written instructions gets a silently ungated/unheadered path, which is a real misconfiguration vector."
