    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "The chain is real. Mochi.ts:898-901 throws `new MochiHttpError(500, formatCompileErrors(compileErrors))` on the page render path with no mode check, and runtime/errors.ts:120 takes a MochiHttpError's status *and message* verbatim (`err instanceof MochiHttpError ? { status: err.status, message: err.message } : { status: 500, message: 'Internal Server Error' }`); only `stack` is dev-gated (errors.ts:93). templates/DefaultError.svelte renders `{error.message}` in both `<title>` and the body, so the text reaches any anonymous visitor in production (escaped, so no XSS — as the finding says). This is a genuine break in the framework's otherwise consistent dev/prod discipline (compare handleEnhancedError at 743, emitError at 527). Two accuracy notes. First, the disclosed content is narrower than 'absolute paths and code frames' for most error kinds: `formatCompileError` runs every file path through `relForDisplay()` (ComponentRegistry.ts:276, 281, 287), so those are project-relative. The exception is `css-bundle-failed`, whose `cssPath` is the resolved entry path and whose `message` is `formatBuildMessages(cssResult.logs)` (line 2205) — raw Bun bundler output, which does carry absolute paths and code frames. Second, reachability requires a broken deploy: either production without a prebuilt manifest (Mochi.ts:607 logs this as unsupported but keeps serving) or a manifest miss compiled on demand at the island endpoint (1612 → compileAll's `loadedFromManifest && !development` warn path at 735, which can still push to `this.errors`). Keeping MEDIUM: conditional, low-sensitivity disclosure (project structure and build diagnostics), but a real missing production guard."
