    "findingId": "F8",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "The mechanics are exactly as described: `createHighlighter` keys on `${language}\\0${code}` (highlight.ts:50) so the full source text is retained in the key, stores the wrapped HTML as the value, and evicts only on `cache.size >= max` with `max` defaulting to 1000 (lines 39, 56-59). There is no byte budget, no TTL, and because it is a plain `Map` rather than a `MochiCache` it is untouched by the `memory:pressure`/`cache:pressure` drain. The comment at 42-43 claiming 'insertion-ordered eviction bounds an app highlighting user content' is the overclaim the finding exposes — the bound is on cardinality, not bytes, so 1000 large distinct submissions pin ~1000 × (source + rendered HTML) of unreclaimable heap, and packages/site pins its highlighter process-wide via `pinGlobal`. Downgrading MEDIUM→BUG for two reasons. First, the title's 'unbounded retention' is imprecise: retention is bounded at `cacheSize` entries, just not at a byte budget, so the worst case is large rather than infinite. Second, exploitability depends entirely on an app feeding untrusted input into a public helper that nothing in this repo does (packages/site highlights only trusted docs/demo code), and the knob to disable it (`cacheSize: 0`) already exists and is documented. It is a sound hardening recommendation (byte budget, hash the key, warn in the docs) rather than a live vulnerability."
