    "findingId": "F9",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "The code path is exactly as reported. mochiConfig.ts:32 computes `envKeyPresent = Boolean(envKey)` with no trim or format validation, so any non-empty string suppresses the `randomBytes(32)` fallback at line 42; `Buffer.from(envKey, 'base64url')` then decodes leniently, silently dropping out-of-alphabet characters, so a value with fewer than two valid base64url characters yields a zero-length buffer, and passphrase-shaped values yield 5-6 bytes (the finding's figures for 'changeme'→6 and 'hunter2'→5 match Node's base64 decoding semantics exactly). Lines 43-49 *detect* the condition (`baseKey.length < 32`, 'likely a typo or truncated value') and then proceed anyway in every mode. The key flows to `payloadCrypto.sivKey()` (line 41), which derives the AES-256-SIV key as `createHmac('sha512', secret).update('mochi-payload-aes-siv-v1')` — with a short secret that is offline-brute-forceable, and since AES-SIV decryption verifies its own tag an attacker with one observed token has a cheap offline verifier. That single key is the whole authorization boundary for server-island props, signed image URLs, captcha tokens, protection clearance cookies, and the public `encryptPayload` export. I confirmed no compensating validation exists anywhere: no `checkEnvironment` check, no app-level `mochi:init` assertion (packages/support enforces ADMIN_PASSWORD/SMTP_HOST/MOCHI_ORIGIN only), and docs/70-server-islands.md:234 plus the payloadCrypto header comment actively advertise that 'any MOCHI_KEY length works'. This is correctly scoped away from the known false-positive (random key when unset is unforgeable; this path is not). One caveat I could not settle statically without running code: for the exact zero-byte case the forgery claim additionally assumes `createHmac` accepts a zero-length key — overwhelmingly likely (RFC 2104 permits any key length and OpenSSL/BoringSSL allow it), but if it threw, the outcome would be a noisy fail-closed error rather than a forgeable key. The short-key case needs no such assumption, so the finding stands at MEDIUM either way."
