    "findingId": "F11",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Confirmed against the installed dependency, not just by inference. `hasSubscribers` (events.ts:532-533) inspects only `mochiEvents.all.get(name)`, but mitt 3.0.1's minified source in node_modules/mitt/dist/mitt.mjs stores every handler — including wildcard ones — in the same `all` map (`on: function(t,e){var i=n.get(t);i?i.push(e):n.set(t,[e])}`) and its `emit` invokes `n.get(\"*\")` handlers on every event. `MochiEmitter extends Emitter<MochiEventMap>`, which includes mitt's `on('*', handler)` overload, so an app subscribing once with `mochiEvents.on('*', …)` — the natural shape for a generic log/metrics forwarder — is reported as having no subscribers for any specific key. compiler/preprocessCache.ts:22 and :32 gate emission on exactly that check, so those two events are silently never emitted for a wildcard-only subscriber, while docs/147-events.md:73-81 presents `hasSubscribers` as the general way to gate expensive payloads. events.test.ts:148-167 only covers typed `.on(name, …)` registration, so nothing catches it. Impact today is limited to two dev-time compile-cache events, which matches the reported BUG severity; the latent trap for future gated emissions is the real reason to fix it, and the suggested one-line fix (also count `all.get('*')`) is correct."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F12",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Verified at every link. queue.ts:938 computes `active` by dropping dev-skipped jobs, 966 builds `declaredQueues` from `active` alone, and 967-971 unschedules every schedule returned by `boss.getSchedules()` that is not in that set. I checked bun-boss: `getSchedules()` with no arguments issues `SELECT * FROM <schema>.schedule ORDER BY name, key` (node_modules/bun-boss/src/plans.ts:971-973 via timekeeper.ts:209), so it returns every schedule in the fixed `mochi_cron` schema with no owner filter — the reconcile's notion of 'no longer declared' is evaluated against a store the docs explicitly tell you to share. docs/118-scheduled-jobs.md confirms both preconditions: the warning callout instructs multi-node deployments to 'point `cronStorage` at shared storage — Postgres, or a SQLite file on a shared volume', the options list defines `dev: false` as merely 'skip the job when `development: true`', and the reconcile is described as removing 'any schedule **it manages**' — an ownership scope the implementation does not have. Both failure modes hold: a `development: true` boot against shared storage unschedules exactly the `dev: false` production jobs fleet-wide (and dev/devWatcher.ts:379 re-runs the same destructive reconcile with `development: true` on every cron-array edit), and two distinct Mochi apps sharing one Postgres database mutually wipe each other's schedules on every boot — that second case needs no misconfiguration at all, since `CRON_SCHEMA` is a hard-coded constant. The failure is silent (an `unschedule` call, no error) and durable until a declaring boot re-registers. HIGH_BUG is justified by the no-misconfiguration multi-app case plus the silent, persistent loss of scheduled work."
