    "findingId": "F13",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Confirmed by reading `createWorker` in full (queue.ts:1105-1195). `started` is declared at 1137; `start()` throws 'Mochi.worker(): this worker was already started.' when it is set (1140-1142), sets it at 1149, and correctly rolls it back in the catch at 1177 — so the variable is clearly intended to mean 'currently started'. `stop()` (1181-1193) only loops over the queues calling `boss.offWork(...)` and deleting their `workIds`; it never resets `started`. The handle is therefore permanently inert after a stop: no worker is registered, yet `start()` rejects with a message that is actively misleading in that state. docs/115-queues.md:146 documents `worker.stop()` as deregistering the queues 'while the runtime stays up for producing' and never says the handle is single-use, so a pause/resume lifecycle is a reasonable reading; the only overstatement in the finding is 'wedges silently' — it throws rather than failing quietly. BUG is the right severity: a real state-machine asymmetry with a one-line fix (reset `started` at the end of `stop()`), affecting only an uncommon pause/resume pattern."
